[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The “invisible bridge” is an OSPF virtual link between the ABRs r4 and r8, carried across area 1 to tie r8’s side back into the backbone (area 0). That link is currently healthy: both endpoints show a Full adjacency over VLINK0 (r4: neighbor 18.1.4.1 on VLINK0; r8: neighbor 14.1.4.1 on VLINK0), and end‑to‑end reachability between the user networks (pc0 ↔ pc12) is normal. Configs on both routers explicitly define the virtual link (r4: area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1; r8: area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1).

Mechanism: when either r4 or r8 restarts, the virtual link drops until OSPF forms again. Because all inter‑area routing must traverse area 0, losing this single virtual link temporarily partitions the topology and makes the “far half” vanish from the routing tables.

Localization: OSPF process/adjacency on r4 (RID 14.1.4.1) and r8 (RID 18.1.4.1), interface VLINK0 via area 1.

Root cause: transient loss of the sole OSPF virtual link between r4 and r8 during a device/service restart, exposing a single point of failure in the design.

Corrective fix: No breakage persists now. To prevent recurrence, either replace the virtual link with a real backbone path (a physical area‑0 connection between the halves) or add redundancy (e.g., a second area‑0 path or alternate virtual link). At minimum, ensure FRR/OSPF autostarts on boot and monitor the VLINK0 adjacency so any future drop is alerted immediately.
=== END ===